course: dictionary
lesson: 12187
title: One word, more than one meaning
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. To pick the right one, read the words around it: they are its context. «The bakery window was full of colourful confections.» The bakery and the word colourful tell you that confections are sweet treats, such as cakes and chocolates.
explain: Confection can also mean something fancy and decorated, like a dress or a building. «Her wedding dress was a confection of lace and ribbons.» You cannot eat a dress, so the clues show that confection here means something delicate and very decorated.
explain: To check your meaning, swap it into the sentence and see if it still makes sense. Confectionery means sweets and chocolates, and a confectioner is a person who makes or sells them. «The confectioner made cakes for the whole village.»
fact: Context means the words and sentences around a word.
fact: A confection is a sweet food, such as a cake, a sweet, a chocolate or a pudding, especially a fancy one.
fact: Confection can also mean something elaborate and decorated, such as a fancy dress or a building.
fact: Confectionery means sweets and chocolates. A confectioner makes or sells them.
fact: In a formal book, confection can mean the making of something by mixing. Sweet confections are a treat, not a meal.
exercise: «The bakery window was full of colourful confections.» What are confections in this sentence? | *Sweet foods, such as cakes and chocolates | A type of bird | A kind of rain | A bus timetable
exercise: «Her wedding dress was a confection of lace and ribbons.» What does confection mean here? | A muddy puddle | A plain, simple T-shirt | *Something fancy and decorated | A sweet snack
exercise: Mum says sweet confections are «a treat, not a meal». What should you think of them as? | A main meal for every day | Something to read before bed | *Something nice to have now and then | A kind of bread
test: «The confectioner made cakes for the whole village.» Who is a confectioner? | A person who sleeps all day | *A person who makes or sells sweets and cakes | A person who teaches maths | A person who mends roads
test: In a formal book, confection can mean the making of something by mixing. Which meaning fits best? | Running very fast | Hiding something from view | *Making something by mixing | A tidy room
exam: Which sentence shows that confection means something fancy and decorated? | «It rained all day on Tuesday.» | «The shop sold sweets and chocolate.» | *«Her wedding dress was a confection of lace and ribbons.» | «We ate our lunch at noon.»
